 "standard_text": "2A.5C rewrite exponential equations as their corresponding logarithmic equations and logarithmic equations as their corresponding exponential equations",
 "meta_desc": "Free Algebra II logarithm worksheets for Texas TEKS 2A.5C: exponential and logarithmic form, evaluating logs, properties, change of base and estimates. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "log_b x = y means b^y = x.",
 "key_sub": "A logarithm is an exponent.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>log<sub><i>b</i></sub> <i>x</i> = <i>y</i> means <i>b</i><sup><i>y</i></sup> = <i>x</i>. A logarithm is an exponent.</li><li>log means base 10; ln means base <i>e</i>.</li><li>log<sub><i>b</i></sub> <i>b</i><sup><i>n</i></sup> = <i>n</i> and <i>b</i><sup>log<sub><i>b</i></sub> <i>x</i></sup> = <i>x</i>.</li><li>Product: log(<i>MN</i>) = log <i>M</i> + log <i>N</i>. Quotient: subtract. Power: log <i>M</i><sup><i>k</i></sup> = <i>k</i> log <i>M</i>.</li><li>Change of base: log<sub><i>b</i></sub> <i>x</i> = ln <i>x</i> ÷ ln <i>b</i>.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Write 3<sup>4</sup> = 81 in log form</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>The base stays 3; the exponent is the answer</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: log<sub>3</sub> 81 = 4</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>log<sub>2</sub> 50</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>2<sup>5</sup> = 32, 2<sup>6</sup> = 64</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: Between 5 and 6</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Swapping the base and the exponent</td><td>log<sub>3</sub> 81 = 4 means 3<sup>4</sup> = 81.</td></tr><tr><td>Dividing logs instead of subtracting</td><td>log <i>M</i> − log <i>N</i> = log(<i>M</i> ÷ <i>N</i>).</td></tr><tr><td>Dividing by the base</td><td>log<sub>2</sub> 50 is not 25.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What does TEKS 2A.5C ask?",
   "a": "Rewrite exponential equations as their corresponding logarithmic equations and logarithmic equations as their corresponding exponential equations."
  },
  {
   "q": "Can you take the log of a negative number?",
   "a": "No, not as a real number. The input must be positive."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is log 1?",
   "a": "0 in every base, because b⁰ = 1."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why is ln useful?",
   "a": "It is the inverse of e^x, which models continuous growth."
  }
